// anim.cpp, frame data and the pure renderer for the DOWN page.
//
// The bitmaps are carried over verbatim from the old breadboard 2x2 clock
// (~/flowclock/oled2x2x-ref/justclockc3). Generated by the Wokwi animator,
// artwork by icons8. 48x48 at 288 bytes a frame, 111 frames, ~32 KB of flash
// against ~900 KB free, the size objection people expect here does not apply.
//
// ===========================================================================
// WHY THE FRAME TABLES ARE UNSIZED, AND WHY EACH ONE IS static_assert-ED
// ===========================================================================
// heartFrames used to be declared
//
//     #define HEART_FRAME_COUNT 28
//     static const uint8_t PROGMEM heartFrames[HEART_FRAME_COUNT][288] = { ... };
//
// with TWENTY-SEVEN initialisers in the braces. That is not an error in C++  - 
// it is a defined, silent zero-fill. heartFrames[27] became 288 bytes of zeros,
// anim_count(AN_HEART) went on returning 28, and anim_draw's `tick % n` reached
// it once per cycle. Every 28 frames, which at ANIM_FRAME_MS = 125 is every
// 3.5 seconds, the heart rendered as NOTHING and came back the next frame. The
// user reported it as "sometimes skips a frame". It was.
//
// Changing 28 to 27 would fix that one instance and leave the mechanism intact.
// So instead:
//
//   1. The first extent is EMPTY. The compiler counts the initialisers; there
//      is no second number for them to disagree with.
//   2. The count is DERIVED, sizeof(x)/sizeof(x[0]), never typed.
//   3. A static_assert PINS it. Adding or dropping a frame is now a compile
//      error that says so, rather than a blank frame nobody sees for a month.
//
// And a fourth lock lives outside this file: tools/layoutcheck renders every
// tick of every animation and fails on any frame that lights zero pixels,
// unless anim_blank_ok() declares that the animation wants one. Its selftest
// plants a blanked heart frame and must catch it.
